# The issue: when closing triple string, after i=19 (= n), we set in_triple_double = true
# We need to check `i == n` differently: if we exited with break (found closing triple), 
# i would be 19 but that's also n. The condition `i == n` can't distinguish.
# 
# Better fix: use a flag variable, OR change the loop condition.

# The issue: `i + 2 < n` prevents matching the LAST 3 chars.
# For row "    \"\"\"docstring\"\"\"" (n=19):
# Last 3 chars are at positions 16, 17, 18 (indices)
# When i=16: i+2 = 18, n=19, so 18 < 19 = true -> match!
# After match: i = 19 = n
# After loop: i == n -> sets in_triple_double = true

# But we DID close it! The bug is that `i == n` is true both when:
# 1. The string was closed and happened to end at position n-3 (i.e., the closing triple is at the end)
# 2. The string was NOT closed (ran to end of row without closing)

# The fix: use the condition `i > n` (impossible - i can't exceed n in the while loop)
# OR: change detection of closing to `i + 2 <= n - 1` = `i + 2 < n`
# That's already what we have for DETECTION, but i becomes n after the break

# The REAL fix: set i = n+1 when we find the closing triple, to distinguish from "ran out"
# After close: i = 19 + 1 = 20 > n = not equal to n
# After exhausted: i = 19 = n = equal to n

